---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TimelineItem from "../components/TimelineItem.astro";
---

<Layout title="About — Adam Richie-Halford" description="Background and experience of Adam Richie-Halford, technology leader at Stanford University.">
  <section class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6 py-24 sm:py-32">
    <div class="max-w-3xl">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 mb-8">
        <picture>
          <source srcset="/images/headshot.webp" type="image/webp" />
          <img
            src="/images/headshot.jpg"
            alt="Adam Richie-Halford"
            width="80"
            height="80"
            loading="eager"
            class="w-20 h-20 rounded-full object-cover ring-2 ring-slate-800 ring-offset-2 ring-offset-slate-950 flex-shrink-0 scale-110" style="object-position: 30% 20%;"
          />
        </picture>
        <div>
          <p class="text-sm font-mono text-accent mb-1 tracking-wide">About</p>
          <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold text-slate-50">
            Builder, scientist, leader.
          </h1>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-5 text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          I lead engineering at
          <a href="https://ed.stanford.edu/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-light transition-colors">Stanford University</a>
          for
          <a href="/roar" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-light transition-colors">ROAR</a> —
          a K-12 reading-assessment platform now deployed in 309 districts, 2,700+ schools,
          and used by 160K+ K-12 students. I own the technical strategy, the engineering team, and
          the data and safety practices that make a research platform something a school district can
          trust for daily classroom use. The work spans hiring, roadmap, architecture, vendor
          and contractor management, FERPA-grade data governance, and budget. The job is to build the
          backbone for national scale without losing the rigor that makes the assessments worth
          deploying in the first place.
        </p>
        <p>
          A focus of mine right now is what AI changes for a small engineering team. I treat AI as a
          force multiplier across the engineering lifecycle — coding agents, AI code review on every
          PR, generative testing, and a domain-knowledge documentation layer that grounds every
          AI-assisted change. Inside the product itself, my focus is psychometric integrity:
          validated assessments grounded in modern measurement theory — computerized adaptive
          testing, latent-ability models, IRT item banks — with validation work in partnership with
          the
          <a href="https://acceleratelearning.stanford.edu/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-light transition-colors">Stanford Accelerator for Learning</a>
          and results published in peer-reviewed journals. Getting both halves right matters more
          when your users are children: it's the same set of safety, privacy, and reliability
          questions, just with the bar set higher.
        </p>
        <p>
          Before this role I was a postdoc at the
          <a href="https://escience.washington.edu/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-light transition-colors">UW eScience Institute</a>,
          where I built open-source tools that ended up in real research workflows: pyAFQ (Nature
          Methods), Cloudknot (SciPy), Groupyr (JOSS), and AFQ-Insight (PLOS Computational Biology).
          My PhD is in computational physics from the University of Washington, where I ran
          Monte Carlo simulations on DOE supercomputers as a
          <a href="https://www.krellinst.org/csgf/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-light transition-colors">Computational Science Graduate Fellow</a>.
          That work produced a Physical Review Letters paper and the kind of habit of correctness
          you only build when an experimentalist is going to compare your numbers to the universe.
        </p>
        <p>
          Before grad school I served as a
          <a href="https://www.peacecorps.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-light transition-colors">U.S. Peace Corps</a>
          health-education volunteer in Morocco, designing federally funded youth programs in rural
          schools and co-organizing the country's first national English spelling bee. Before that
          I was a U.S. Air Force officer and systems engineer at the
          <a href="https://www.losangeles.af.mil/About-Us/Fact-Sheets/Article/343702/space-and-missile-systems-center/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-light transition-colors">Space and Missile Systems Center</a>,
          where I program-managed an $8M software platform and helped link a $2.4B lab budget to a
          $12B satellite R&D portfolio. Different problems, same shape: own a complex technical
          program, get it across the finish line, write the report yourself.
        </p>
        <p>
          The thread through all of it is building things that are rigorous enough for science
          <em>and</em> practical enough for real-world deployment, on small teams, where every
          person has to be senior in some direction. That's the only kind of engineering culture
          I know how to lead.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 pt-10 border-t border-slate-800/50">
        <h2 class="text-xs font-mono text-slate-400 uppercase tracking-widest mb-6">What I Own</h2>
        <ul class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-x-8 gap-y-3 text-sm text-slate-300">
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="text-accent mt-0.5">▸</span>
            Engineering strategy, technical roadmap, and operating cadence
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="text-accent mt-0.5">▸</span>
            Hiring, mentorship, and team structure (currently six engineers)
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="text-accent mt-0.5">▸</span>
            AI-accelerated engineering practice — tooling, QA, evaluation
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="text-accent mt-0.5">▸</span>
            End-to-end architecture: web platform, APIs, data pipelines, integrations
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="text-accent mt-0.5">▸</span>
            Reliability discipline: monitoring, incident response, release management
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="text-accent mt-0.5">▸</span>
            Data governance, privacy &amp; user safety on a K-12 platform (FERPA-aligned)
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="text-accent mt-0.5">▸</span>
            Technology budget, vendor selection, and contractor management
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="text-accent mt-0.5">▸</span>
            Cross-functional partnership with Product, Research, and 25+ school districts
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-slate-800/50 py-20">
    <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6">
      <h2 class="text-xs font-mono text-slate-400 uppercase tracking-widest mb-12">How I Work</h2>
      <div class="max-w-3xl space-y-8 text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-100 mb-2">AI is a force multiplier inside engineering</h3>
          <p class="text-base text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            On small teams, AI is the difference between a roadmap that ships and one that
            slips. I treat coding agents, generative testing, and AI code review as part of
            the engineering workbench — but the highest-leverage work is the scaffolding
            around them: domain-knowledge documentation, project conventions, and review
            heuristics that determine whether AI accelerates the team or distracts it.
            The bar is higher when the users are kids.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-100 mb-2">80% of the value with 20% of the effort</h3>
          <p class="text-base text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Building for the future isn't the same as over-engineering for the present. I
            push the team to find the smallest version of the right thing and ship it,
            then earn the right to iterate. Most of the wins on platforms I've led came
            from saying no to the tempting overbuild.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-100 mb-2">Speed and rigor are not opposites</h3>
          <p class="text-base text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Reliability is a feature. Monitoring, audit logs, CI/CD, and a clean incident
            cadence don't slow a team down — they're how a small team avoids spending its
            time fighting the same fire twice. The discipline pays for itself inside a
            single quarter.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-100 mb-2">Engineering and Product is a partnership</h3>
          <p class="text-base text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            The healthiest teams I've been part of run on creative tension between an
            engineering leader who owns <em>how</em> and a product leader who owns
            <em>what</em> and <em>why</em>. I prefer to be jointly accountable, argue
            it out in the room, and present a single recommendation to the rest of the
            org.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-100 mb-2">Privacy and safety are design constraints, not paperwork</h3>
          <p class="text-base text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Working on platforms that serve children sets the floor. Least-privilege
            access, system-of-record clarity, retention/deletion as code, and vendor
            review aren't compliance theater — they're the kind of choices that look
            paranoid right up until the moment they save you.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-slate-800/50 py-20">
    <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-6">
      <h2 class="text-xs font-mono text-slate-400 uppercase tracking-widest mb-12">Timeline</h2>
      <div class="max-w-2xl">
        <TimelineItem
          year="2023–present"
          title="Director of Technology & Innovation"
          org="Stanford University — ROAR / Graduate School of Education"
          detail="Leading technical development of web-based reading assessments and data infrastructure."
        />
        <TimelineItem
          year="2022–2023"
          title="Research Scientist"
          org="Stanford University — ROAR / Developmental-Behavioral Pediatrics"
          detail="Data science, neuroimaging, and educational technology."
        />
        <TimelineItem
          year="2020–2022"
          title="Data Science Postdoctoral Fellow"
          org="University of Washington — eScience Institute"
          detail="Built open-source tools for reproducible neuroimaging analysis."
        />
        <TimelineItem
          year="2014–2020"
          title="PhD, Nuclear Physics"
          org="University of Washington"
          detail="Computational Science Graduate Fellowship. Quantum Monte Carlo simulations on DOE supercomputers."
        />
        <TimelineItem
          year="2011–2013"
          title="Peace Corps Volunteer"
          org="U.S. Peace Corps — Kingdom of Morocco"
          detail="Health education, community infrastructure, and peer education programs."
        />
        <TimelineItem
          year="2006–2010"
          title="Air Force Officer & Systems Engineer"
          org="U.S. Air Force — Space and Missile Systems Center"
        />
        <TimelineItem
          year="2010"
          title="MS, Condensed Matter Physics"
          org="California State University, Long Beach"
          detail="Graduate Dean's List (top 1.5%)."
        />
        <TimelineItem
          year="2006"
          title="BS, Engineering Physics"
          org="Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University"
          detail="Summa Cum Laude."
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</Layout>
